  "title": "Past, continuous and perfect up close",
  "lesson": {
    "kind": "deepen",
    "focusPoint": 1,
    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Point 1: Review of the last class",
        "body": "What did you learn in the last class?\n\nLast class you met five tenses: one word and more words. Today we study three of them on the Mérida cable car."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Point 2: Simple past",
        "body": "You left the first station of the Mérida cable car. How do you say it?\n\n“I sang”: the action is finished. It is a station you already left. Add -ed (walked) or change: sing, sang."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Point 3: Past continuous",
        "body": "While the Mérida cable car climbed, you sang for a long time. Which tense?\n\nUse was or were + a verb with -ing. “I was singing”, “they were singing”. It is the ride between two stations."
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "heading": "Point 4: Present perfect",
        "body": "You are at the top station now. How do you say what you have done?\n\nUse have or has + a past participle. “I have sung”: you are at the top now. The result is still here today."
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "heading": "Point 5: The past participle",
        "body": "I have walked, but I have sung. Why? How do you make the past participle?\n\nRegular verbs add -ed: walk, walked. Irregular: sing, sang, sung. Also: eat, ate, eaten."
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "heading": "Point 6: Have or has?",
        "body": "You say “I have sung”. What about Ana? Is it “Ana have sung” or “Ana has sung”?\n\nI, you, we and they use have. He, she and it use has. “Ana has sung” is correct."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "I sang is finished, I was singing was going on, and I have sung links before with today.",
    "memoryPhrase": "Simple past: I sang. Past continuous: I was singing. Present perfect: I have sung.",
    "weekRecap": "One word: canto / cantaba / canté / cantaré / cantaría. Two words: helper haber + participle (he cantado). Learn to narrate WHEN an action happens: one-word forms and two-word forms, choosing with meaning.",
    "layout": "letter-grid-v2",
    "v2Flow": true,
    "imageBandInstruction": "Draw three cards in a row. Write I sang, I was singing and I have sung. Under each card, leave a blank line for your own sentence with that tense.",
